t would be a flat list. For our simulation, round1 applications: halve-a, drop-b, drop-c (all three made changes). round2: only halve-a. So total applications = [\"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\", \"halve-a\"]. That is plausible.\n\nBut why would the output require \"applications\"? In many rewriting systems, you want to know which rules fired. The output contract says \"applications\" array of strings. The task may be to simulate the process and record the names of passes that performed a replacement in the order they are applied (i.e., whenever a pass does a replacement, its name is appended to the list). At the end, we output that list. That would be a straightforward interpretation.\n\nAlso note that the input payload has \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore. The fixture has three identical payloads, so the expected output should be the same for all. That suggests a deterministic mapping.\n\nLet's simulate carefully with Python. We'll write solve(case) that:\n- Initialize text = case[\"text\"]\n- passes = case[\"passes\"]\n- max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n- applications = []\n- For round in range(max_rounds):\n    changed_in_round = False\n    for p in passes:\n        old, new = p[\"old\"], p[\"new\"]\n        if old in text:\n            text = text.replace(old, new)\n            applications.append(p[\"name\"])\n            changed_in_round = True\n    if not changed_in_round:\n        break\n- fixed_point = (changed_in_round == False)  # But we break when no change, so after loop, if we broke because no change, fixed_point = True. If we exhausted max_rounds without reaching fixed point, fixed_point = False? Actually the loop breaks when no change. If we used all max_rounds and still changed in the last round, then we exit loop because max_rounds reached, and fixed_point = False because we haven't reached a fixed point (there might be further changes). So we need to track.\nBetter: after the loop, fixed_point = (changed_in_round == False) only if we broke early. If we finished all rounds without breaking, then fixed_point = False. So we can set a flag.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p[\"old\"]\n            new = p[\"new\"]\n            # Since replace all occurrences, we should check if old in text before? but replace itself handles it.\n            if old in text:\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(p[\"name\"])\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    # If loop completed without break, fixed_point remains False\n    return {\"text\": text, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"applications\": applications}\n\nBut is this correct? For our simulation, we got:\nRound1: changed (all three). text becomes \"aa b\" after round? Wait earlier I computed round1 gave \"aa b\"? Let's recalc:\nStart: \"aaaa bb cc\"\nPass1: \"aa\" -> \"a\": \"aaaa\".replace() -> \"aa\". So text = \"aa bb cc\"? Actually after replace: \"aa bb cc\" (since \"aaaa\" -> \"aa\" and rest unchanged). Then pass2: \"bb\" -> \"b\": \"aa bb cc\".replace(\"bb\",\"b\") -> \"aa b cc\". Then pass3: \" cc\" -> \"\": \"aa b cc\".replace(\" cc\",\"\") -> \"aa b\". So round1 text = \"aa b\". applications in round1: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\"].\nRound2: \"aa b\". Pass1: \"aa\" -> \"a\": \"aa b\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") -> \"a b\" (changed). applications add \"halve-a\". Pass2: \"bb\" not in \"a b\", skip. Pass3: \" cc\" not in \"a b\", skip. So changed=True, text becomes \"a b\". applications now [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"].\nRound3: start \"a b\". Pass1: \"aa\" not in, skip. Pass2: \"bb\" not in, skip. Pass3: \" cc\" not in, skip. So changed=False, fixed_point=True, break. Return text=\"a b\", fixed_point=True, applications = [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"].\n\nThat matches the intuitive list.\n\nNow check if there is any nuance: The passes are applied in order, and we only apply a pass if its old string exists in the current text. The replace is done globally. That's standard. We record the pass name each time it actually makes a replacement.\n\nThus the implementation is as above.\n\nWe must output as JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code string. Ensure proper escaping. The source code should be a function definition. Use only standard library.\n\nEdge cases: case may have other keys; ignore them. Ensure we don't mutate case.\n\nWrite the code.\n\nNow produce the final response with the envelope: exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value string of Python source.\n\nWe'll write the source as a single line? Better to have readable with newlines escaped. In JSON string, newlines are \\n. We'll format the code as a multi-line string within JSON.\n\nThe implementation string should be valid Python. We'll use triple quotes? No, it's a JSON string, so we can include newlines as \\n, but we can also include actual newlines in the JSON string? JSON allows newlines in string? Actually JSON string cannot contain literal newlines; they must be escaped as \\n. So we'll use \\n. But easier to write the code as a single line? That's messy. We'll write with \\n.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the code using triple quotes inside the Python source? But the outer JSON string will have backslashes for quotes. Better to keep code simple.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import copy\\n    text = case['text']\\n    passes = case['passes']\\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\\n    applications = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for round in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            old = p['old']\\n           